Hi Eric,

I've been following this with interest, as I'm grappling with a similar problem. Presumably, assuming a common detection function across species is problematic only if the true detectability varies among those species? In an ongoing analysis, I've fitted a detection function by pooling three closely-related species, which are morphologically and behaviourally very similar. In order to check the assumption that detectability is similar across species, I have compared models with and without species as a covariate. Based on AIC, the latter perform just as well as the former. This is helpful, because in my case degrees of freedom are very limited and much needed for other covariates which do have a large effect on the detection function. Can you foresee any problems with this approach?

All the best,

Ewan

