Dear Eric, Ewan and All,

Taking Ewan's approach of AIC model comparison, would it be possible to systematically group species in this way? My interpretation is that if two species have similar histograms of detections, they are likely to share a detection function(?) In that case, rather than the crude pooling of species by maximum detection distance as I started out with, I could pool species with similar detection histograms. I can see this approach will be laborious in my case with 250 species, but would at least be consistent. First build a group of species with visually similar detection patterns, and then use AIC to contrast models with and without species as a covariate. If species covariate doesn't add anything to the model then I can assume the pooled group is legitimate.

Even if I end up with 15 - 20 groups that would be much better than 250 individual species parameters-wise. I recognise this wouldn't work for species with low numbers of detections, (I would pool those in a "low-detections" group), but should be ok for those with sufficient detections to give the expected detectability curve. Does this sound ok?

Kind regards,
Tom

